WEBVTT

NOTE Visual text from a silent video.

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:07.400
An aggregate volume does not describe a basin’s local pressure by itself.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:14.100
The same withdrawal matters more when less water is available.

3
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:22.000
Compare location, period and competing uses, not just national totals.

4
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:29.400
Withdrawal is the water taken from a source into the system.

5
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:36.100
Some may evaporate; some may leave as blowdown requiring treatment.

6
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:44.000
Distinguish inputs, consumption and return before comparing facilities.

7
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:51.400
WUE relates water consumption to energy delivered to computing equipment.

8
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:58.100
For the same 1 MWh, 2 L/kWh means 2,000 litres; 0.2 L/kWh means 200.

9
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:08.000
These are facility examples, not a universal water cost per query.

10
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:15.400
PUE divides total facility energy by the energy used by IT equipment.

11
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:22.100
For 1 MWh of IT energy, lowering PUE from 2 to 1.2 reduces the total to 1.2 MWh.

12
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:32.000
If the IT load grows to 3 MWh, the same PUE gives 3.6 MWh in total.

13
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:39.400
Chips, interconnects, batteries and supporting systems need different materials.

14
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:46.100
Extraction, refining and manufacturing add impacts outside the data center itself.

15
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:54.000
Launching the equipment does not remove that earlier supply chain.

16
00:01:54.700 --> 00:02:01.400
Count manufacturing, operation, repair, reuse and end of life.

17
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:08.100
Reusing components can extend service without eliminating every impact.

18
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:16.000
Compare alternatives for the same useful work over the complete lifecycle.

